#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120576} :activity/id #uuid "ed79a46b-23ff-5442-970b-42e684476845" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051045 :topic/id #uuid "7cf45c34-fae0-53a3-89c2-bd091d1c7405" :topic/math-academy-id 1838} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121001 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2ecbf26-2299-5d4f-9ca0-ac626810c1ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2375 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3cac974a-1992-543d-93ed-f87c6e569e32" :step/math-academy-id 10294 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119561 :question/id #uuid "9b2026e3-781b-5e73-8dc2-0e8f2789fe67" :question/math-academy-id "e-3065"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef2c4b9b-cf73-56d1-96b1-73f48b50ed7b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443548}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length Given Its Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "316716f0-90d4-5cb3-8194-1e5c03222fc9" :step/math-academy-id 10295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119562 :question/id #uuid "74f33486-6391-5ec0-8a5b-bb51c9af2d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3066"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aecbb8b-bda3-5956-9b0f-98509330a84d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048418}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348078} {:db